.TH "CROSS\-PLATFORM FILENAME GLOBBING." "3" "" "pstring 1.5" "pstring manual"
.SH NAME
Cross\-platform filename globbing. \- pstrglob_match implements POSIX fnmatch(3) style matching of a single name against a pattern (, ?, [...], and [!...]/[^...]), with no filesystem access at all \- it's a pure string predicate and behaves identically on every platform.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
\fB#include <glob.h>\fR
.fi
.PP
.nf
\fBint pstrglob_match(
    const char *pattern, const pstring_t *name, int flags
);\fR
.fi
.PP
.nf
\fBint pstrglob(
    pstrarray_t *dst, const pstring_t *pattern, int flags, allocator_t *alloc
);\fR
.fi
.PP
.nf
\fBint pstrbrace(
    pstrarray_t *dst, const pstring_t *pattern, allocator_t *alloc
);\fR
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
\fBpstrglob_match\fR implements POSIX \fBfnmatch(3)\fR style matching of a single
name against a pattern (\fB*\fR, \fB?\fR, \fB[...]\fR, and \fB[!...]\fR/\fB[^...]\fR), with
no filesystem access at all \- it's a pure string predicate and behaves
identically on every platform.
.PP
\fBpstrglob\fR walks a path pattern component by component (splitting on
\fB/\fR), listing directories with \fB<dirent.h>\fR on POSIX and
\fBFindFirstFile\fR/\fBFindNextFile\fR on Windows, and matching each component
against the corresponding pattern segment with \fBpstrglob_match\fR. Matches
are collected depth\-first, so a pattern with multiple wildcard segments
(e.g. \fBsrc\fR then \fB*\fR then \fB*.c\fR) works the same way it would in a
POSIX shell.
.SH REFERENCE
.SS pstrglob_match
.PP
Tests whether \fBname\fR matches the shell\-style wildcard \fBpattern\fR:
.IP \(bu 2
\fB*\fR matches any run of characters (including none),
.IP \(bu 2
\fB?\fR matches exactly one character,
.IP \(bu 2
\fB[abc]\fR matches one of \fBa\fR, \fBb\fR, or \fBc\fR,
.IP \(bu 2
\fB[a\-z]\fR matches one character in the given range,
.IP \(bu 2
\fB[!...]\fR or \fB[^...]\fR matches any character NOT in the set,
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\e\ex\fR matches the literal character \fBx\fR (unless \fBPSTRGLOB_NOESCAPE\fR).
.PP
Unlike \fBpstrglob\fR, this function never touches the filesystem, so it
is safe to use as a general\-purpose wildcard matcher (e.g. for the
\fBcase\fR patterns during word expansion).
.PP
\fBReturns\fR
.PP
\fBPSTRING_TRUE\fR, \fBPSTRING_FALSE\fR, or a negative \fBpstring_error\fR.
.SS pstrglob
.PP
Expands \fBpattern\fR into every path that exists on disk and matches it,
appending results to \fBdst\fR (which follows the same ownership rules as
\fBpstrarray_t\fR elsewhere in pstring: initialize with \fB{0}\fR and free with
\fBpstrarray_free\fR). Path components are separated by \fB/\fR on every
platform; \fB\e\e\fR is additionally accepted as a separator on Windows.
.PP
If no matches are found, returns \fBPSTRING_ENOENT\fR unless
\fBPSTRGLOB_NOCHECK\fR is set, in which case \fBpattern\fR itself is appended
as the sole result.
.PP
\fBErrors\fR
.PP
EINVAL, ENOMEM, ENOENT.
.SS pstrbrace
.PP
Expands shell\-style \fB{a,b,c}\fR brace notation, appending every
resulting combination to \fBdst\fR (same ownership rules as \fBpstrglob\fR:
initialize with \fB{0}\fR, free with \fBpstrarray_free\fR). This is a purely
textual expansion \-\- it never touches the filesystem and has nothing
to do with \fBpstrglob_match\fR's wildcards:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
"file.{c,h}"     \-> ["file.c", "file.h"]
"a{b,c{d,e}}f"   \-> ["abf", "acdf", "acef"]
"img{01..03}.png"  (numeric ranges are not supported)
.fi
.RE
.PP
A \fB{...}\fR group is only expanded if it contains at least one
top\-level comma; otherwise its braces are left as literal characters
(matching the shell convention that \fB{lonely}\fR is not a valid brace
expression). \fB\e\e{\fR, \fB\e\e}\fR and \fB\e\e,\fR are literal characters rather
than group/separator syntax. Multiple groups in the same pattern
(adjacent or nested) all expand, and the results are the cross
product of every group's alternatives.
.PP
\fBErrors\fR
.PP
EINVAL, ENOMEM.
